//! lotus/stereo_invert.rye — ALES127: stereo_invert — the phase flip of a master carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES105 named `invert` — flip every sample's sign, y =
//! saturate(-x), the whole clip mirrored about the zero line — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and
//! RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries invert into stereo the way ALES126 carried rotate: run ALES105's proven mono `invert` on each channel,
//! so a phase flip that brings two mics on one source into agreement, cancels a difference signal, or folds a stereo side lands in BOTH speakers at once. It lifts the value-mirror of the stereo
//! suite's PURE REARRANGEMENT class — where reverse (ALES125) and rotate (ALES126) trade every seat and touch no value, invert flips every value and touches no seat, the exact complement — and
//! leaves only the parity flip (nyquist_flip) to complete the class; like reverse and rotate it is TOTAL and needs no validation.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_invert(sc) flips both channels about zero, reusing ALES105's mono `invert` per channel over its public API. Because mono invert is total (it names no span, so it
//! raises no fault) and holds each channel's length, the balance invariant is preserved trivially — both channels enter equal-length, both flip in place, both hold their length, so they leave
//! equal-length; there is no fault to forward, no EditError, no `try`. A stereo master's phase is inverted as one thing, both speakers together.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO VALUE-MIRROR LAW: the left channel equals mono invert(left) and the right equals mono invert(right), each byte for byte against a hand-inverted vector, sample
//! i holding saturate(-x) per channel, every value flipped and no seat moved. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length — invert flips
//! values, never the count, so the stereo image stays aligned. THE INVOLUTION LAW, WITH AN HONEST RAIL: stereo_invert twice returns both channels to the byte EXCEPT the two's-complement floor —
//! an sample_min (-32768) seat negates to +32768, saturates to +32767, and round-trips to -32767 per channel, the fixed asymmetry saturated by design, every other seat round-tripping exactly on
//! both channels. THE MAGNITUDE / FIXPOINT LAW: off the rail |-x| = |x|, so a channel whose peak is not sample_min holds its peak magnitude (re-measured through ALES13); zero is the unique fixed
//! point of negation, so an all-zero pair and an empty pair are each their own invert.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES127.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-024705_fill-ales127-lotus-stereo-invert.md
//! Stands on: lotus/invert.rye (ALES105) — the mono sign flip run once per channel, itself reusing ALES2's one true `saturate`; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips,
//! left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and its buf/len/samples; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used by the witness to prove a sign flip
//! off the rail moves no loudness in either channel.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It flips existing samples about zero in each channel and fabricates
//! none; it changes no length and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_invert.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const invert = @import("invert.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_invert_version = "20260815.024705";

/// Flip both channels of `sc` about the zero line in lockstep, each sample becoming saturate(-x), the stereo image staying balanced and each channel's length holding. Total on any balanced
/// StereoClip: mono invert raises no fault and holds each length, so the two channels leave equal-length and no refusal can land between them. Reuses ALES105's mono invert per channel, which
/// itself reuses ALES2's one true saturate so the two's-complement floor is clamped in exactly one place.
pub fn stereo_invert(sc: *pan.StereoClip) void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // Mono invert is total and holds the length, so each channel flips in place and no fault can land between the two edits.
    invert.invert(&sc.left);
    invert.invert(&sc.right);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — each held its length under the same flip, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO VALUE-MIRROR LAW — each channel equals ALES105's mono invert on that channel byte for byte, sample i holding saturate(-x); THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len ==
//     right.len after, both holding their starting length; THE INVOLUTION LAW WITH AN HONEST RAIL — twice the identity off the floor, sample_min seats pinning to -32767 on both channels; THE
//     MAGNITUDE / FIXPOINT LAW — each channel's peak unchanged off the rail, an all-zero pair and an empty pair their own invert ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// A channel's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 — the reader the magnitude law checks each channel against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO VALUE-MIRROR LAW — each channel equals ALES105's mono invert on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples; the i16 floor rides
    //    along on both so its saturated negation (+32768 clamps to sample_max) is proven in place.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, -20, 0, 100, -32768 }, &[_]i16{ -11, 21, 32767, -41, 51 });
        stereo_invert(&sc);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, -20, 0, 100, -32768 });
        invert.invert(&ml);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ -11, 21, 32767, -41, 51 });
        invert.invert(&mr);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: the floor -32768 saturates to +32767 on the left; +32767 negates cleanly to -32767 on the right.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -10, 20, 0, -100, 32767 })) return error.LeftInvertWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, -21, -32767, 41, -51 })) return error.RightInvertWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at an even and an odd length.
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3, -4 }, &[_]i16{ -5, 6, -7, 8 });
        stereo_invert(&even);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;
        if (!eq(even.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -1, 2, -3, 4 })) return error.EvenLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(even.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 })) return error.EvenRightWrong;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ -6, 7, -8 });
        stereo_invert(&odd);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(odd.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -1, 2, -3 })) return error.OddLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(odd.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, -7, 8 })) return error.OddRightWrong;
    }

    // 3a. THE INVOLUTION LAW, off the rail — for a pair free of sample_min, stereo_invert twice returns both channels to the byte: the flip is its own inverse.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, -20, 30, -40, 32767 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ -11, 22, -33, 44, -32767 };
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        stereo_invert(&sc);
        stereo_invert(&sc);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.InvolutionLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.InvolutionRightWrong;
    }

    // 3b. THE INVOLUTION LAW, on the rail — a channel holding sample_min does NOT round-trip to the byte: -32768 → 32767 → -32767, so those seats land -32767, the honest two's-complement
    //     asymmetry carried into stereo; every other seat on both channels round-trips exactly.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -32768, 500, -32768, -1 }, &[_]i16{ 7, -32768, 9, -32768 });
        stereo_invert(&sc);
        stereo_invert(&sc);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -32767, 500, -32767, -1 })) return error.RailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -32767, 9, -32767 })) return error.RailRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE MAGNITUDE LAW — off the rail |-x| = |x|, so each channel whose peak is not sample_min holds its peak magnitude re-measured through ALES13; the flip moves the sign, never the
    //     loudness. Peaks 30000 and 32000, both off the floor.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -30000, 5, -5, 30000 }, &[_]i16{ 50, -32000, 70, 5 });
        const lb = try clip_peak(&sc.left);
        const rb = try clip_peak(&sc.right);
        stereo_invert(&sc);
        if (try clip_peak(&sc.left) != lb) return error.LeftPeakMoved;
        if (try clip_peak(&sc.right) != rb) return error.RightPeakMoved;
    }

    // 4b. THE FIXPOINT LAW — zero is the unique fixed point of negation, so an all-zero pair and an empty pair are each their own invert (-0 = 0).
    {
        var empty = pan.StereoClip{};
        stereo_invert(&empty);
        if (empty.left.len != 0 or empty.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;

        var zeros = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zeros, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 });
        stereo_invert(&zeros);
        if (!eq(zeros.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.ZerosLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(zeros.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.ZerosRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-invert: STEREO_INVERT — the phase flip of a master, both channels in lockstep. Flip both channels about zero, reusing ALES105's mono invert per channel; a Lotus " ++
        "master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so a phase flip that brings two mics into agreement or cancels a difference " ++
        "signal lands in both speakers at once. It lifts the value-mirror of the stereo suite's pure rearrangement class — where reverse and rotate trade every seat and touch no value, invert " ++
        "flips every value and touches no seat — leaving only the parity flip (nyquist_flip) to complete the class; like reverse and rotate it is total and needs no validation. The crux is " ++
        "four laws. THE STEREO VALUE-MIRROR LAW: each channel equals ALES105's mono invert on that channel byte for byte, sample i holding saturate(-x), every value flipped and no seat moved. " ++
        "THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVOLUTION LAW, WITH AN HONEST RAIL: " ++
        "stereo_invert twice returns both channels to the byte except the two's-complement floor — an sample_min (-32768) seat negates to +32768, saturates to +32767, round-trips to -32767 " ++
        "per channel, the fixed asymmetry saturated by design. THE MAGNITUDE / FIXPOINT LAW: off the rail each channel's peak re-measured through ALES13 is unchanged (the flip moving the sign " ++
        "not the loudness), an all-zero pair and an empty pair each their own invert. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips flipped about zero through the proven mono invert, reading no byte " ++
        "past either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
